## PART 2: Revised Proposals

### REVISED MCU Proposal — RP2350 + FreeRTOS

```
# Proposal: MCU Secure Boot & Immutable Trust Chain

**Subproject:** MCU Firmware (flipperone-mcu-firmware)
**Risk Level:** High (bootloader modification, brick risk)
**Estimated Effort:** 6–8 weeks
**Dependencies:** RP2350 bootrom, FreeRTOS, team alignment on security model

---

## Hardware Reality

Flipper One MCU uses the **Raspberry Pi RP2350** (dual-core Cortex-M33, FreeRTOS), not STM32U5. This changes the trust chain architecture:

- **Bootrom:** RP2350 factory bootrom supports verified boot with RSA-2048 signatures
- **TrustZone:** Cortex-M33 optional ARM TrustZone — available if team chooses to enable
- **FreeRTOS:** Application runtime — no hardware abstraction layer like STM32 HAL
- **Open source:** Firmware is fully open — security model must preserve developer freedom

---

## Revised Trust Chain

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Stage 0: RP2350 Bootrom (immutable)          │  ← Factory ROM
│  Verifies: Stage 1 signature (RSA-2048)       │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Stage 1: Second Stage Bootloader (SSBL)      │  ← Project-controlled
│  Verifies: Stage 2 (FreeRTOS firmware) sig    │
│  Anti-rollback: OTP monotonic counter         │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Stage 2: FreeRTOS Main Firmware              │  ← Standard firmware
│  Runtime attestation: Periodic hash checks    │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Stage 3: Flipper Apps (updatable)            │  ← User applications
│  Optional: App-level signature verification   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

---

## Key Differences from STM32U5 Draft

| Aspect | STM32U5 (old draft) | RP2350 (revised) |
|--------|---------------------|------------------|
| Bootrom | STM32 factory ROM | RP2350 ROM with RSA verify |
| Secure storage | STM32U5 TrustZone | RP2350 OTP + optional TrustZone |
| RTOS | Bare metal / custom | FreeRTOS |
| Key format | ECDSA / Ed25519 | RSA-2048 (bootrom native) |
| Developer freedom | Dev key flashable | Dev key flashable (preserved) |

---

## Key Management (Open Source Model)

| Key | Purpose | Storage |
|-----|---------|---------|
| Project Public Key | Verify official firmware | Embedded in SSBL |
| Project Private Key | Sign release binaries | Offline, CI/CD secret |
| User Public Key | Verify custom/dev firmware | User-flashable via UF2 |
| Device Unique Key | Attestation, identity | RP2350 OTP region |

**Sovereignty preserved:** Users can flash their own public key to boot custom firmware. The project key only verifies "official" builds.

---

## Implementation Path

**Phase 1:** QEMU/RP2350 simulator — validate boot flow without hardware
**Phase 2:** Pico 2 dev board — validate FreeRTOS + bootrom integration
**Phase 3:** Flipper One hardware — integrate with existing UF2 update system
**Phase 4:** CI/CD signing pipeline — automate release binary signing

---

## Files

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `ssbl.c` | Second stage bootloader with RSA verification |
| `anti_rollback.c` | OTP monotonic counter management |
| `runtime_attest.c` | Periodic firmware hash verification |
| `key_storage.c` | OTP / TrustZone secure storage interface |
| `signing_tool.py` | CI/CD firmware signing (RSA-2048) |
| `verify_tool.py` | Host-side signature verification |
| `threat_model.md` | Full threat model and mitigations |
| `README.md` | Integration and build guide |

---

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] Bootrom rejects unsigned firmware with clear error message
- [ ] Rollback to older version blocked by OTP counter
- [ ] Runtime attestation detects single-bit firmware corruption
- [ ] Custom firmware possible with user-flashed dev key
- [ ] Brick recovery: BOOTSEL button always forces UF2 mode

---

ΩV — AEGENTIS Security Command
```

