### REVISED Constellation Proposal — Standard Protocols Only

```
# Proposal: Constellation Mesh Networking Protocol

**Subproject:** Linux Build Scripts / Networking (flipperone-linux-build-scripts)
**Risk Level:** Low (userspace networking, no firmware changes)
**Estimated Effort:** 4–6 weeks
**Dependencies:** MT7921 WiFi/BLE module, standard Linux networking stack

---

## Hardware Reality

Flipper One has **no Sub-GHz radio**. Wireless is provided by the **MediaTek MT7921**:
- **WiFi:** 802.11a/b/g/n/ac/ax — supports 802.11s mesh
- **Bluetooth:** 5.2 — supports Bluetooth Mesh profile
- **Team preference:** Standard stack, IP-based transport, use-case oriented functionality

---

## Revised Protocol Stack (Standard Only)

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Application Layer                            │  ← RF heatmap, file share, task sync
│  Protocol: HTTPS / CoAP / MQTT over mesh IP   │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Transport Layer                              │  ← Standard protocols only
│  WiFi mesh: 802.11s + batman-adv              │
│  BLE mesh: Bluetooth Mesh (model layer)       │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Network Layer                                │  ← Linux kernel
│  Standard IPv6 / IPv4 routing                 │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Physical Layer                               │  ← MT7921 firmware
│  Existing driver, no custom radio work        │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

---

## No Custom Wire Format

**Removed from original draft:**
- ❌ Custom "CNST" packet structure
- ❌ CBOR encoding
- ❌ Custom discovery beacon
- ❌ Sub-GHz transport

**Using instead:**
- ✅ 802.11s standard mesh beaconing
- ✅ Bluetooth Mesh standard provisioning + model layer
- ✅ IPv6 link-local addressing
- ✅ Standard TLS 1.3 for application security

---

## PoC Scope: Distributed RF Environment Mapping

**Real-world use case (per team preference):**

1. **Discovery:** Devices auto-join 802.11s mesh on boot (SSID: `flipper-mesh-*`)
2. **Data sharing:** Each device runs `flipper rf-scan`, publishes results to mesh-local MQTT broker
3. **Aggregation:** Any device (or host laptop) subscribes to all nodes, builds unified heatmap
4. **No central server:** Mesh is self-forming, self-healing, infrastructure-independent

**Why this use case:**
- Demonstrates mesh value without requiring core team mesh development
- Builds on existing `flipper` CLI tools
- Produces tangible output (heatmap) that validates the architecture

---

## Implementation Path

**Phase 1:** WiFi mesh PoC — 2–4 devices, 802.11s + batman-adv, RF heatmap demo
**Phase 2:** BLE mesh fallback — lower power mode for battery-constrained scenarios
**Phase 3:** Application SDK — library for apps to declare mesh-shared state
**Phase 4:** Integration — `flipperone-linux-build-scripts` includes mesh networking as optional package

---

## Files

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `mesh-setup.sh` | 802.11s + batman-adv auto-configuration |
| `mesh-discovery.py` | mDNS/Avahi peer discovery on mesh |
| `rf-heatmap-aggregator.py` | Subscribes to all nodes, builds heatmap |
| `ble-mesh-setup.sh` | Bluetooth Mesh provisioning script |
| `app_mesh_sdk.h` | C API for apps to publish/subscribe mesh state |
| `README.md` | Protocol spec and integration guide |

---

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] 2+ devices form mesh within 60 seconds of boot
- [ ] RF scan data syncs across mesh within 5 seconds
- [ ] Heatmap renders correctly from multi-node data
- [ ] Mesh heals within 120s of node departure/reappearance
- [ ] Power overhead < 10% of baseline idle (WiFi mesh active)

---

